Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant tables / columns and how they will be used

• ball_by_ball  
  – match_id : to know the match in which every delivery occurred  
  – over_id , ball_id , innings_no : together with match_id these form the “primary key” of a delivery; they let us join to batsman_scored  
  – striker : the batsman on strike for that delivery (player_id) – we will sum his runs across the match  

• batsman_scored  
  – match_id , over_id , ball_id , innings_no : join keys to ball_by_ball  
  – runs_scored : the number of runs credited to the striker off that delivery – we will SUM this value  

• player  
  – player_id : to link to ball_by_ball.striker if we want the batsman’s name (optional)  
  – player_name : readable name of the striker  

Important conditions / logic we will need

1. JOIN ball_by_ball b   ON (b.match_id, b.over_id, b.ball_id, b.innings_no) =  
   batsman_scored s      (s.match_id, s.over_id, s.ball_id, s.innings_no)

2. GROUP BY b.match_id , b.striker        -- total runs of each striker in each match

3. HAVING SUM(s.runs_scored) > 50         -- keep only those “match‑striker” totals > 50

4. Take AVG(total_runs) over all such rows

